      "text": "Given the functions \\( p(x), q(x), \\) and \\( r(x) \\) are all invertible, we need to determine the correct expression for the inverse function \\( f^{-1} \\) of the composition \\( f = q \\circ p \\circ r \\).\n\nFor \\( f = q \\circ p \\circ r \\) to have an inverse \\( f^{-1} \\), the composition of these functions must correspond to a function whose inverse is the direct sequential composition of their inverses.\n\n1. \\( p \\) is invertible by the standard result: it has \\( p^{-1} \\).\n\n2. \\( q \\) is invertible: thus, it has \\( q^{-1} \\).\n\n3. \\( r \\) is invertible by the standard result: it has \\( r^{-1} \\).\n\nNow, let magnify the given functions through the intermediate functions:\n1. When \\( f = q \\circ p \\circ r \\), this can be rewound to express the original sequence:\n   - \\( f \\circ p = \\left( q \\circ p \\circ r \\right) \\circ p = q \\circ r \\circ p \\circ p = q \\circ r \\circ p \\circ p \\triangleq A \\)\n2. \\( A = q \\circ r \\circ p \\circ p \\) represents the original sequence until we have the product:\n\n   - \\(\\left( A = q \\circ r \\circ p \\right) \\circ p \\) corresponds to \\( q \\circ r \\circ p \\).\n3. As we can proceed with the next composition:\n   - \\( f = q \\circ r \\circ p \\circ p \\circ p = q \\circ r \\circ p \\).\n4. This operation corresponds to \\( p \\circ p \\) followed by \\( r \\) and \\( q \\) reversals:\n   - Thus: we can reckon that \\( q^{-1} \\circ r^{-1} \\circ p^{-1} \\).\n\nHence, placing the steps or concluding results manifesting in such sequential cancellation function poses:\n\\[ f^{-1} = q^{-1} \\circ r^{-1} \\circ p^{-1}. \\]\n\nThus, the final answer to the composition function \\( f^{-1} \\) is option \\( F \\). \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{F}\n\\]",
