      "text": "To find the inverse function $f^{-1}(x)$ of the function $f(x) = q(p(r(x)))$, we need to apply the inverse functions in reverse order. The reverse order induces the forward order since $f(p(r)) = q(p(r)) = x$. Applying the reciprocals of the functions in reverse order will reverse the order of the composition of functions.\n\nHere are the steps to find the inverse:\n1. Start with the function $f(x) = q(p(r(x)))$.\n2. Apply the inverse function $r^{-1}$ to both sides: $r^{-1}(f(x)) = r^{-1}(q(p(r(x))))$.\n3. Apply the inverse function $p^{-1}$ to both sides: $p^{-1}(r^{-1}(f(x))) = p^{-1}(q(p(r(x))))$.\n4. Apply the inverse function $q^{-1}$ to both sides: $q^{-1}(p^{-1}(r^{-1}(f(x)))) = q^{-1}(q(p(r(x))))$.\n5. Since $q^{-1}(q(x)) = x$, we get $p^{-1}(r^{-1}(f(x)))$.\n6. Applying $r$ to both sides: $r(p^{-1}(r^{-1}(f(x)))) = f(x)$.\n\nRewriting $r(p^{-1}(r^{-1}(f(x))))$, we see that they are the inverse operations in reverse order:\n\\[\nf^{-1}(x) = r^{-1} \\circ p^{-1} \\circ q^{-1}(x).\n\\]\nThus, the correct expression is \\(\\boxed{C}\\).\n\nTo verify, let's use the sympy library to symbolically verify this inverse relationship.\n```python\nimport sympy as sp\n\n# Define symbols\nx = sp.symbols('x')\n\n# Assume p(x), q(x), r(x) as standard functions or symbols\np, q, r = sp.Function('p'), sp.Function('q'), sp.Function('r')\n\n# Define f(x) = q(p(r(x)))\nf = q(p(r(x)))\n\n# Function to find the inverse by reversing the composition\ndef find_inverse(f):\n    # Reverse composition operation\n    f_inv = r_inverse(f)\n    return f_inv\n\n# Example functions for illustration\ndef r_inverse(y):\n    # Assume r_inverse is defined here\n    return y\n\n# Find the inverse for q(p(r(x)))\n# q(p(r(x))) = y, solving for r(x), we denote it as r_inverse(y)\nr_r_inv = sp.simplify(r_inverse(f))\n\n# Combining it with p(x), assuming p_inverse and q_inverse are known functions\nf_inv = p_inverse * r_inverse\nq_r_inv = q_inverse\np_r_inv = p_inverse\n\n# Display result\nf_inv_result = q_r_inv * p_r_inv * r_r_inv\nprint(f_inv_result)\n```",
